[agent:result] class StatementExecutionAPI(builtins.object)
[agent:result]  |  StatementExecutionAPI(api_client)
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  The Databricks SQL Statement Execution API can be used to execute SQL statements on a SQL warehouse and
[agent:result]  |  fetch the result.
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  **Getting started**
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  We suggest beginning with the [Databricks SQL Statement Execution API tutorial].
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  **Overview of statement execution and result fetching**
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  Statement execution begins by issuing a :method:statementexecution/executeStatement request with a valid
[agent:result]  |  SQL statement and warehouse ID, along with optional parameters such as the data catalog and output format.
[agent:result]  |  If no other parameters are specified, the server will wait for up to 10s before returning a response. If
[agent:result]  |  the statement has completed within this timespan, the response will include the result data as a JSON
[agent:result]  |  array and metadata. Otherwise, if no result is available after the 10s timeout expired, the response will
[agent:result]  |  provide the statement ID that can be used to poll for results by using a
[agent:result]  |  :method:statementexecution/getStatement request.
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  You can specify whether the call should behave synchronously, asynchronously or start synchronously with a
[agent:result]  |  fallback to asynchronous execution. This is controlled with the `wait_timeout` and `on_wait_timeout`
[agent:result]  |  settings. If `wait_timeout` is set between 5-50 seconds (default: 10s), the call waits for results up to
[agent:result]  |  the specified timeout; when set to `0s`, the call is asynchronous and responds immediately with a
[agent:result]  |  statement ID. The `on_wait_timeout` setting specifies what should happen when the timeout is reached while
[agent:result]  |  the statement execution has not yet finished. This can be set to either `CONTINUE`, to fallback to
[agent:result]  |  asynchronous mode, or it can be set to `CANCEL`, which cancels the statement.
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  In summary: - **Synchronous mode** (`wait_timeout=30s` and `on_wait_timeout=CANCEL`): The call waits up to
[agent:result]  |  30 seconds; if the statement execution finishes within this time, the result data is returned directly in
[agent:result]  |  the response. If the execution takes longer than 30 seconds, the execution is canceled and the call
[agent:result]  |  returns with a `CANCELED` state. - **Asynchronous mode** (`wait_timeout=0s` and `on_wait_timeout` is
[agent:result]  |  ignored): The call doesn't wait for the statement to finish but returns directly with a statement ID. The
[agent:result]  |  status of the statement execution can be polled by issuing :method:statementexecution/getStatement with
[agent:result]  |  the statement ID. Once the execution has succeeded, this call also returns the result and metadata in the
[agent:result]  |  response. - **[Default] Hybrid mode** (`wait_timeout=10s` and `on_wait_timeout=CONTINUE`): The call waits
[agent:result]  |  for up to 10 seconds; if the statement execution finishes within this time, the result data is returned
[agent:result]  |  directly in the response. If the execution takes longer than 10 seconds, a statement ID is returned. The
[agent:result]  |  statement ID can be used to fetch status and results in the same way as in the asynchronous mode.
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  Depending on the size, the result can be split into multiple chunks. If the statement execution is
[agent:result]  |  successful, the statement response contains a manifest and the first chunk of the result. The manifest
[agent:result]  |  contains schema information and provides metadata for each chunk in the result. Result chunks can be
[agent:result]  |  retrieved by index with :method:statementexecution/getStatementResultChunkN which may be called in any
[agent:result]  |  order and in parallel. For sequential fetching, each chunk, apart from the last, also contains a
[agent:result]  |  `next_chunk_index` and `next_chunk_internal_link` that point to the next chunk.
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  A statement can be canceled with :method:statementexecution/cancelExecution.
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  **Fetching result data: format and disposition**
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  To specify the format of the result data, use the `format` field, which can be set to one of the following
[agent:result]  |  options: `JSON_ARRAY` (JSON), `ARROW_STREAM` ([Apache Arrow Columnar]), or `CSV`.
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  There are two ways to receive statement results, controlled by the `disposition` setting, which can be
[agent:result]  |  either `INLINE` or `EXTERNAL_LINKS`:
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  - `INLINE`: In this mode, the result data is directly included in the response. It's best suited for
[agent:result]  |  smaller results. This mode can only be used with the `JSON_ARRAY` format.
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  - `EXTERNAL_LINKS`: In this mode, the response provides links that can be used to download the result data
[agent:result]  |  in chunks separately. This approach is ideal for larger results and offers higher throughput. This mode
[agent:result]  |  can be used with all the formats: `JSON_ARRAY`, `ARROW_STREAM`, and `CSV`.
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  By default, the API uses `format=JSON_ARRAY` and `disposition=INLINE`.
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  **Limits and limitations**
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  Note: The byte limit for INLINE disposition is based on internal storage metrics and will not exactly
[agent:result]  |  match the byte count of the actual payload.
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  - Statements with `disposition=INLINE` are limited to 25 MiB and will fail when this limit is exceeded. -
[agent:result]  |  Statements with `disposition=EXTERNAL_LINKS` are limited to 100 GiB. Result sets larger than this limit
[agent:result]  |  will be truncated. Truncation is indicated by the `truncated` field in the result manifest. - The maximum
[agent:result]  |  query text size is 16 MiB. - Cancelation might silently fail. A successful response from a cancel request
[agent:result]  |  indicates that the cancel request was successfully received and sent to the processing engine. However, an
[agent:result]  |  outstanding statement might have already completed execution when the cancel request arrives. Polling for
[agent:result]  |  status until a terminal state is reached is a reliable way to determine the final state. - Wait timeouts
[agent:result]  |  are approximate, occur server-side, and cannot account for things such as caller delays and network
[agent:result]  |  latency from caller to service. - To guarantee that the statement is kept alive, you must poll at least
[agent:result]  |  once every 15 minutes. - The results are only available for one hour after success; polling does not
[agent:result]  |  extend this. - The SQL Execution API must be used for the entire lifecycle of the statement. For example,
[agent:result]  |  you cannot use the Jobs API to execute the command, and then the SQL Execution API to cancel it.
[agent:result]  |
[agent:result]  |  [Apache Arrow Columnar]: https://arrow.apache.org/overview/
[agent:result]  |  [Databricks SQL Statement Execution API tutorial]: https://docs.databricks.com/sql/api/sql-execution-tutorial.html
